// test-execution
// #1484 / ADR-029: a LOCAL variable whose declared type names a function-as-type
// emits that function's `_fp` typedef, exactly as a parameter and a scope member
// already did. Only the local path never asked, so this emitted
// `onTick handler = onTick;` -- the function's own name in type position, which
// no typedef declares. gcc rejected it and the transpiler exited 0.
//
// Two defects, and the second was hidden behind the first: the type was not
// mapped, AND nothing recorded that a local names a callback type, so even the
// corrected `onTick_fp` referenced a typedef nothing emitted. Both are still
// exercised here: the typedef must exist and the body must use it.
//
// #1491 moved WHERE it exists. It used to go in the header, because "this file
// names the type" was the whole test for header ownership. That put a type in
// the public interface because one function body happened to use it, and it is
// not what C does: a library header typedefs the callback types its own API
// uses -- POSIX's signal-handler typedef, `curl_write_callback` -- while `stdlib.h` writes
// `qsort`'s comparator inline, since no caller needs to name it. It was also
// unsafe: two files that both named an INCLUDED function-as-type locally each
// exported the same typedef, and anything including both headers saw it twice,
// which C99 rejects. So a type named only by a local now lives in the `.c`.
// The header stays empty of it here, deliberately -- nothing in this file's
// public interface mentions either callback type.
//
// The scope case additionally depends on #1472: before that fix the analyzer
// refused the call through `handler` with E0422.
u32 total <- 0;

void onTick(u32 ms) {
    total <- total + ms;
}

scope Motor {
    public u32 ticks <- 0;

    public void onBeat(u32 n) {
        this.ticks <- this.ticks + n;
    }

    // Local variable typed by a scope-declared function-as-type, named bare.
    public void runLocal() {
        onBeat beat <- onBeat;
        beat(7);
    }
}

u32 main() {
    // Top-level: the reported case.
    onTick handler <- onTick;
    handler(5);
    if (total != 5) return 1;

    // Calling through the variable a second time must accumulate, proving the
    // pointer is real and not a no-op.
    handler(3);
    if (total != 8) return 2;

    // Scope-declared function-as-type, reached from a local inside its scope.
    Motor.runLocal();
    if (Motor.ticks != 7) return 3;

    // A `for` init is a FOURTH declaration site: `forVarDecl` is its own
    // grammar rule, so it is not a `variableDeclaration` and was missed by the
    // first fix -- leaving `for (onTick f <- onTick; ...)` emitting the bare
    // function name one line below a corrected declaration, which is the
    // divergent-path shape rather than a leftover. Calling through `f` also
    // exercises the analyzer half, which tracked the other two forms only.
    for (onTick f <- onTick; total < 20; total <- total + 1) {
        f(2);
    }
    if (total != 20) return 4;

    return 0;
}
